// @run
// @log 1065353217
// @log 1065353218
// @log 1036831949
// @log 1036831949
// THE DOUBLE-ROUNDING PIN, and the reason `x as f32` is NOT the oracle for what the
// contextual f32 literal must lower to.
//
// A contextual f32 literal is CONTEXT-TYPED: in an f32 context the literal IS f32 from
// birth and `ieeeBytes` performs a SINGLE round-to-nearest-ties-to-even on the exact
// decimal value. It never routes decimal -> f64 -> f32. That distinction is not
// theoretical, and this file is its witness.
//
//   1.00000017881393432617187
//
// sits just BELOW the f32 midpoint between 1 + 2^-23 (0x3F800001, odd significand) and
// 1 + 2^-22 (0x3F800002, even). Rounded ONCE at f32 it goes DOWN to 0x3F800001. Rounded
// through f64 first it lands exactly ON the midpoint (the decimal is within a half-ulp of
// f64 there), and the second rounding then applies ties-to-EVEN and goes UP to 0x3F800002.
// Two different answers from the same source text.
//
// `as f32` takes the second path by construction and correctly so — it is an explicit
// conversion of an f64 VALUE, and it emits `f64.const` + `f32.demote_f64`. So the cast
// twin of a contextual literal is not merely a different encoding of the same number: for
// this input it is a DIFFERENT NUMBER, and byte-identity with it would be a regression,
// not the acceptance criterion.
//
// For `0.1` the two paths agree, which is exactly why the disagreement needs a
// constructed witness rather than a convenient one.
const single: f32 = 1.00000017881393432617187
print(f32bits(single))

const doubled: f32 = 1.00000017881393432617187 as f32
print(f32bits(doubled))

// The agreeing control: nothing about the ordinary case changes.
const tenthBare: f32 = 0.1
print(f32bits(tenthBare))
const tenthCast: f32 = 0.1 as f32
print(f32bits(tenthCast))
